  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "TRIAL EDITION - SQL longer than 10,000 characters is rejected (parse() returns -1). Full edition: https://www.sqlparser.com\n\nRelease 4.1.0.10\n\n- [DB2] Accept an empty compound statement body, BEGIN END, and a body\n  made up only of DECLARE statements. This is what makes the common\n  idiom for swallowing an error legal, for example\n  DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '23505' BEGIN END. Applies to\n  procedure bodies, trigger bodies, SQL function bodies and handler\n  blocks, with or without a label and with or without [NOT] ATOMIC.\n- [DB2] Accept the AUTONOMOUS option in CREATE PROCEDURE, which runs\n  the procedure in its own transaction scope. AUTONOMOUS is not a\n  reserved word and stays usable as a column or table name.\n- [DB2] Fix statement splitting inside routine bodies. A script whose\n  procedure, trigger or function contained any of the following was\n  split at the wrong place, so the statement that followed the routine\n  was merged into it and the results were wrong without any error\n  being reported:\n    * a nested block used as a statement, BEGIN ... END; inside the body\n    * a cursor declared FOR a select or FOR a prepared statement name\n    * a call to the scalar function REPEAT(), for example REPEAT('x',3)\n    * a loop closed with an end label, END WHILE L1 / END LOOP L1 /\n      END REPEAT L1\n    * a column labelled END, for example SELECT x AS END FROM t\n  Known remaining limitation: the bare alias form SELECT x END FROM t,\n  without AS, is still split incorrectly.\n\nDemos: https://github.com/sqlparser/gsp_demo_dotnet",
